1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,407
"The tests passed on your laptop and
failed in CI on the same commit.

2
00:00:08,447 --> 00:00:09,781
Was it the same commit?"

3
00:00:10,121 --> 00:00:14,055
"The build stamped the release
as 6fe5455 and not as a version.

4
00:00:14,095 --> 00:00:15,443
Who removed the tags?"

5
00:00:15,783 --> 00:00:20,712
Two questions from a CTO, and one cause.
CI is the automated testing that runs on

6
00:00:20,752 --> 00:00:23,903
each change. The answer to
the first question is no.

7
00:00:23,943 --> 00:00:28,811
It wasn't the same commit, and the commit
CI tested doesn't exist in your clone,

8
00:00:28,851 --> 00:00:32,738
your copy of the repository. The
answer to the second is nobody.

9
00:00:32,778 --> 00:00:36,052
The tags, fixed names for
commits, were never fetched.

10
00:00:36,392 --> 00:00:41,295
Both follow from the defaults of one action
that is the first step of almost every

11
00:00:41,335 --> 00:00:44,369
job. So how can CI test a
commit that nobody wrote?

12
00:00:44,409 --> 00:00:49,433
Keep that question. In this video you rebuild
what that action leaves on the runner,

13
00:00:49,473 --> 00:00:51,603
with plain Git, on your own machine.

14
00:00:53,443 --> 00:00:57,401
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:00:57,441 --> 00:01:02,776
GitHub Actions is a service that reacts to
events on GitHub by running commands on

16
00:01:02,816 --> 00:01:08,150
machines. A job is a list of steps on one
fresh machine, the runner, and an action

17
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:13,525
is a packaged, reusable step. Video 142
said that Git appears in exactly one place

18
00:01:13,565 --> 00:01:18,900
there: when a job fetches commits onto the
runner. This video is about that place.

19
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:24,626
The layers are worth separating. A ref is
a name that holds an object ID, such as a

20
00:01:24,666 --> 00:01:29,790
branch or a tag. Which ref and which
options the checkout action uses is GitHub

21
00:01:29,830 --> 00:01:35,020
Actions, taken from the action's README
and its definition at version 7.0.1, the

22
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:40,315
version pinned in this course. What a
repository with one commit and no tags then

23
00:01:40,355 --> 00:01:45,676
answers to git describe or git log is Git,
and it behaves the same on a runner and

24
00:01:45,716 --> 00:01:46,460
on your Mac.

25
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:51,638
So the demonstration can be local. A bare
repository, one with no working tree,

26
00:01:51,678 --> 00:01:56,763
stands in for GitHub. One honest limit,
which the textbook states: the runner's own

27
00:01:56,803 --> 00:02:01,518
sequence of Git commands is an implementation
detail and isn't what is shown.

28
00:02:01,558 --> 00:02:04,667
What is shown is the
documented result, reproduced.

29
00:02:05,007 --> 00:02:09,976
There are two halves. One commit and no
tags: the shallow clone and what it does to

30
00:02:10,016 --> 00:02:14,925
history questions. Then the merge ref:
which commit a pull request run checks out.

31
00:02:14,965 --> 00:02:19,089
A pull request is GitHub's proposal
to merge one branch into another.

32
00:02:20,929 --> 00:02:22,507
Here's what you'll walk away with.

33
00:02:22,847 --> 00:02:24,259
After this video you can:

34
00:02:24,599 --> 00:02:28,311
say what the runner's repository
contains after a default checkout;

35
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:33,190
name three commands or tools that give
wrong results there and the minimal fix for

36
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:33,469
each;

37
00:02:33,809 --> 00:02:38,480
explain which commit a pull_request run
checks out and why it is not in your clone;

38
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:42,133
reproduce the runner's checkout
locally with a shallow fetch;

39
00:02:42,473 --> 00:02:44,976
decide when full history
is worth its cost.

40
00:02:46,816 --> 00:02:50,562
In one sentence. A runner starts
with no repository at all.

41
00:02:50,602 --> 00:02:55,438
actions/checkout fetches one commit,
without tags, for the ref of the event.

42
00:02:55,478 --> 00:03:00,763
On pull_request events that ref is the test
merge commit refs/pull/N/merge, checked

43
00:03:00,803 --> 00:03:06,024
out with a detached HEAD. Detached means
that HEAD holds a commit ID, not a branch

44
00:03:06,064 --> 00:03:06,344
name.

45
00:03:06,684 --> 00:03:11,338
Precisely. The inputs, meaning the settings
a workflow passes to this action, and

46
00:03:11,378 --> 00:03:12,207
their defaults.

47
00:03:12,547 --> 00:03:17,528
ref: the ref or commit of the event. On
pull_request, that is refs/pull/N/merge.

48
00:03:17,868 --> 00:03:22,778
fetch-depth: 1. The README: "Only a
single commit is fetched by default".

49
00:03:22,818 --> 00:03:26,236
Zero fetches all history
for all branches and tags.

50
00:03:26,576 --> 00:03:31,199
fetch-tags: false. No tags, so
nothing for git describe to find.

51
00:03:31,539 --> 00:03:36,566
persist-credentials: true. The job token,
the short-lived credential every job gets

52
00:03:36,606 --> 00:03:41,205
for its repository, is kept for later
git commands of the job and removed in

53
00:03:41,245 --> 00:03:46,333
post-job cleanup. Since version 6 it's stored
under the runner's temporary directory

54
00:03:46,373 --> 00:03:51,339
and not in .git/config. The course workflows
set it to false, because none of them

55
00:03:51,379 --> 00:03:56,467
runs an authenticated git command after the
checkout, and a credential that isn't on

56
00:03:56,507 --> 00:03:58,604
disk can't be read by a later step.

57
00:03:58,944 --> 00:04:04,946
lfs and submodules: false. So Git LFS
pointers, the small stand-ins for large files,

58
00:04:04,986 --> 00:04:10,054
aren't replaced by content. And
submodule directories, which hold other

59
00:04:10,094 --> 00:04:11,780
repositories, are empty.

60
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:16,722
clean: true, which matters on self-hosted
runners, machines you operate yourself,

61
00:04:16,762 --> 00:04:18,040
that reuse a workspace.

62
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:23,513
And allow-unsafe-pr-checkout: false.
Version 7 refuses to check out fork pull

63
00:04:23,553 --> 00:04:27,274
request code under
pull_request_target and workflow_run.

64
00:04:27,314 --> 00:04:30,700
A fork is a second, connected
repository on GitHub.

65
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:32,044
Part 7 explains why.

66
00:04:32,384 --> 00:04:37,096
Inside .git. Every commit names its
parents, the commits it was built on.

67
00:04:37,136 --> 00:04:41,784
A shallow fetch writes the fetched
commit's ID to the file .git/shallow.

68
00:04:41,824 --> 00:04:47,058
That file tells Git to treat the commit
as having no parents, although the commit

69
00:04:47,098 --> 00:04:50,703
object itself still names
them. No tag refs are created.

70
00:04:50,743 --> 00:04:55,391
HEAD is a branch for push events and
a bare commit ID for pull requests.

71
00:04:55,731 --> 00:05:00,621
Think about what "no parents" does. Every
command that walks history starts at HEAD,

72
00:05:00,661 --> 00:05:05,493
reaches the cut, and stops. git describe
walks parents looking for a tagged commit.

73
00:05:05,533 --> 00:05:09,131
git log for a path looks for the
commit that changed the file.

74
00:05:09,171 --> 00:05:13,709
git merge-base looks for a common ancestor.
None of them can see past the cut.

75
00:05:14,049 --> 00:05:18,941
Why it does this. The textbook's reason is
a fair one: a job that only compiles and

76
00:05:18,981 --> 00:05:23,516
tests needs the snapshot, not the history.
Fetching one commit is the fastest

77
00:05:23,556 --> 00:05:28,389
correct default for that job. The default
is right for most jobs and wrong for the

78
00:05:28,429 --> 00:05:30,350
ones that ask history a question.

79
00:05:30,690 --> 00:05:35,691
The merge ref. Chapter 17 showed that GitHub
keeps, for a mergeable pull request, a

80
00:05:35,731 --> 00:05:40,247
test merge commit under refs/pull/N/merge.
The events reference states that

81
00:05:40,287 --> 00:05:45,349
pull_request workflows set GITHUB_REF to
that ref and GITHUB_SHA to that commit, and

82
00:05:45,389 --> 00:05:50,269
that the checkout action therefore, in the
documentation's words, "checks out the

83
00:05:50,309 --> 00:05:55,188
merge branch", so that "CI tests run against
the merged result, not just the head

84
00:05:55,228 --> 00:05:56,039
branch alone".

85
00:05:56,379 --> 00:06:01,378
Why? The textbook: the question a pull
request asks is "is the result of merging

86
00:06:01,418 --> 00:06:05,410
safe", which only the merged tree,
the merged files, can answer.

87
00:06:05,750 --> 00:06:08,381
Three consequences, all
from the same pages.

88
00:06:08,721 --> 00:06:13,775
First, the test merge can be older than
main. It's regenerated when the pull request

89
00:06:13,815 --> 00:06:18,626
branch is pushed, when the merge base
changes, or when it's older than 12 hours.

90
00:06:18,666 --> 00:06:22,810
The merge base is the most recent
commit that both histories contain.

91
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:27,600
And a re-run of a job reuses the original
commit, so a re-run doesn't pick up a

92
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:28,267
newer base.

93
00:06:28,607 --> 00:06:33,424
Second, a conflicted pull request has no
merge ref, and its pull_request workflows

94
00:06:33,464 --> 00:06:38,340
don't run at all. The symptom is missing
checks, the results reported for a commit,

95
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:39,288
not failed ones.

96
00:06:39,628 --> 00:06:44,225
Third, to test the head commit alone, the
README documents setting ref to the head

97
00:06:44,265 --> 00:06:48,975
commit of the pull request from the event
payload. You then test something that will

98
00:06:49,015 --> 00:06:53,442
never be on the base branch. Do it
knowingly, for example for a linter, a style

99
00:06:53,482 --> 00:06:56,326
checker, that should judge
only the author's files.

100
00:06:56,666 --> 00:07:01,968
When full history is worth its cost. The
rule from the root-cause box: decide per

101
00:07:02,008 --> 00:07:06,914
job whether it asks history a question.
A job that runs unit tests doesn't.

102
00:07:06,954 --> 00:07:12,124
A job that computes a version from tags,
generates a changelog, detects changed

103
00:07:12,164 --> 00:07:17,532
files, or runs blame-based analysis does.
For those, fetch-depth: 0, as workflow 3

104
00:07:17,572 --> 00:07:17,994
has it.

105
00:07:19,834 --> 00:07:20,807
A picture helps.

106
00:07:21,147 --> 00:07:26,147
Analogy, from the textbook. You asked a
courier for "the contract" and received the

107
00:07:26,187 --> 00:07:30,822
last page, unstapled, with a cover sheet
that merges your draft and the other

108
00:07:30,862 --> 00:07:35,741
party's latest draft. It's the right thing
to sign and the wrong thing to consult

109
00:07:35,781 --> 00:07:37,562
about the history of clause 4.

110
00:07:37,902 --> 00:07:42,593
The last page is the single commit. The
cover sheet that merges two drafts is the

111
00:07:42,633 --> 00:07:43,235
test merge.

112
00:07:43,575 --> 00:07:48,400
The analogy breaks in one place: the last
page here is a complete snapshot of every

113
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:51,389
file. Only the history is
missing, not the content.

114
00:07:51,429 --> 00:07:55,843
That's why compiling and testing work
perfectly, and why the failures are so

115
00:07:55,883 --> 00:07:56,370
specific.

116
00:07:56,710 --> 00:08:01,647
Carry one distinction through the demonstration:
does a tool fail, or does it lie?

117
00:08:01,687 --> 00:08:04,742
A tool that fails gives you
an error and a red job.

118
00:08:04,782 --> 00:08:08,869
A tool that lies gives you a confident
wrong answer and a green job.

119
00:08:08,909 --> 00:08:13,603
You'll see one of each, and one that
fails or lies depending on a single flag.

120
00:08:15,443 --> 00:08:19,810
Here's the whole story as one picture.
First main, with its new commit.

121
00:08:19,850 --> 00:08:23,845
Then your branch, from the older
commit. So where is CI's commit?

122
00:08:23,885 --> 00:08:26,701
There: a merge of the
two, on the server only.

123
00:08:27,041 --> 00:08:32,073
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Copy
this drawing, then put one finger on the

124
00:08:32,113 --> 00:08:36,412
commit your laptop tested, and another
on the commit the runner tested.

125
00:08:38,752 --> 00:08:42,470
Your laptop tested 80c9cfa,
the tip of your branch.

126
00:08:42,510 --> 00:08:46,523
The runner tested d04ef31,
the merge commit below main.

127
00:08:46,863 --> 00:08:51,588
Read the root-cause line: the
actions/checkout defaults, fetch-depth 1 and

128
00:08:51,628 --> 00:08:52,682
fetch-tags false.

129
00:08:53,022 --> 00:08:57,924
And the second root cause: the base branch
gained a commit that changes behavior the

130
00:08:57,964 --> 00:08:59,042
new code relies on.

131
00:09:00,882 --> 00:09:01,642
Into the lab.

132
00:09:01,982 --> 00:09:03,658
Step 1: your clone.

133
00:09:03,998 --> 00:09:08,474
Three commits and one annotated
tag, v0.1.0, on the middle commit.

134
00:09:08,514 --> 00:09:12,238
An annotated tag also records
who tagged, when and why.

135
00:09:12,278 --> 00:09:17,233
git describe prints the tag, one commit
after it, and the abbreviated ID.

136
00:09:17,573 --> 00:09:21,865
Here's that clone as a picture: three
commits in a row, the tag on the middle one,

137
00:09:21,905 --> 00:09:22,869
main on the newest.

138
00:09:23,209 --> 00:09:25,319
Step 2: the runner's clone.

139
00:09:25,659 --> 00:09:31,743
git clone is 🟢 SAFE: it creates a new
directory. HUB is a file:// URL of the bare

140
00:09:31,783 --> 00:09:38,095
repository that plays GitHub. The URL form
matters: a plain path would make Git copy

141
00:09:38,135 --> 00:09:40,665
the repository and ignore --depth.

142
00:09:41,005 --> 00:09:46,785
Predict the five outputs: the log, shallow
or not, the content of .git/shallow, the

143
00:09:46,825 --> 00:09:49,730
tags, the commit count. Say them out loud.

144
00:09:54,570 --> 00:09:58,456
One line of log, with the word
"grafted" in the decoration.

145
00:09:58,496 --> 00:10:02,516
Shallow: true. The file holds
the full ID of that one commit.

146
00:10:02,556 --> 00:10:05,045
The tag list is empty. The count is 1.

147
00:10:05,385 --> 00:10:09,661
"Grafted" and the ID in .git/shallow say
the same thing: history is cut here.

148
00:10:09,701 --> 00:10:14,202
In the picture, the two older commits are
hatched: they aren't in this clone, and

149
00:10:14,242 --> 00:10:15,491
the tag went with them.

150
00:10:15,831 --> 00:10:18,364
Step 3: asking history questions.

151
00:10:18,704 --> 00:10:22,617
For each of these three commands:
does it fail, or does it lie?

152
00:10:22,657 --> 00:10:23,934
Make your prediction.

153
00:10:28,774 --> 00:10:33,824
git describe --tags fails:
"No names found", status 128.

154
00:10:33,864 --> 00:10:36,733
A red job. Annoying, and honest.

155
00:10:37,073 --> 00:10:42,994
With --always it doesn't fail. It prints
6fe5455, status 0, and a build stamps that

156
00:10:43,034 --> 00:10:47,232
as the version. That's the CTO's
second question, answered.

157
00:10:47,272 --> 00:10:49,889
One flag turned a failure into a lie.

158
00:10:50,229 --> 00:10:55,635
The git log line is the worst of the three.
It claims that pom.xml was last changed

159
00:10:55,675 --> 00:11:00,556
by the only commit there is, because a
parentless commit "adds" every file.

160
00:11:00,596 --> 00:11:04,165
The true answer, coming in a
moment, is another commit.

161
00:11:04,205 --> 00:11:09,480
Any tool that asks history a question gets
a confident wrong answer: changed-file

162
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:14,729
detection, blame-based analysis, changelog
generators, version-from-tag plugins.

163
00:11:15,069 --> 00:11:19,541
And git merge-base fails, because the
name it was given can't be resolved here.

164
00:11:19,881 --> 00:11:22,370
Step 4: tags alone are not enough.

165
00:11:22,710 --> 00:11:27,630
git fetch is 🟢 SAFE. This is what
fetch-tags: true with depth 1 amounts to.

166
00:11:27,970 --> 00:11:32,999
Quick quiz. The tag will now
exist. Does git describe work?

167
00:11:33,039 --> 00:11:35,319
A, yes. B, no. Your answer?

168
00:11:40,159 --> 00:11:45,128
B. The tag arrived, and describe still fails,
with a different message: "No tags can

169
00:11:45,168 --> 00:11:45,724
describe".

170
00:11:46,064 --> 00:11:50,462
The walk from HEAD through parents ends
at the graft before it reaches the tagged

171
00:11:50,502 --> 00:11:53,312
commit. Tags need the history
between them and HEAD.

172
00:11:53,352 --> 00:11:55,449
If you chose A, you're in good company.

173
00:11:55,789 --> 00:11:57,557
Step 5: full history.

174
00:11:57,897 --> 00:12:02,119
🟢 SAFE: it adds objects and tag
refs and removes .git/shallow.

175
00:12:02,159 --> 00:12:05,969
This is fetch-depth: 0 as a
repair of an existing clone.

176
00:12:06,309 --> 00:12:12,107
Not shallow, three commits, the right
description. And the true answer for pom.xml:

177
00:12:12,147 --> 00:12:15,624
ae299cb, "Add Maven module
for price calculation".

178
00:12:15,964 --> 00:12:20,477
And in the picture the cut is gone: three
commits again, and the tag within reach of

179
00:12:20,517 --> 00:12:20,748
HEAD.

180
00:12:21,088 --> 00:12:29,067
Step 6: the lab replay, briefly.
labs/run ch20a/lab-26-3-describe.

181
00:12:29,407 --> 00:12:33,820
The same commit described three times:
in your repository, in a clone like the

182
00:12:33,860 --> 00:12:36,846
default checkout, and in a
clone like fetch-depth: 0.

183
00:12:36,886 --> 00:12:38,730
The middle answer is the bare ID.

184
00:12:39,070 --> 00:12:42,961
Step 7: the merge ref. Replay
labs/run ch20a/merge-ref.

185
00:12:43,001 --> 00:12:48,607
The fixture: your branch adds a reorder
report whose test assumes the low-stock

186
00:12:48,647 --> 00:12:53,110
threshold is 5. A colleague raised
the threshold to 10 on main.

187
00:12:53,150 --> 00:12:58,257
The two changes touch different files,
so they merge without a conflict.

188
00:12:58,597 --> 00:13:02,284
On your branch, at
80c9cfa, the tests pass.

189
00:13:02,624 --> 00:13:07,409
The server is a bare repository in which
the fixture created the two pull request

190
00:13:07,449 --> 00:13:10,685
refs with plain Git, imitating
the documented behavior.

191
00:13:11,025 --> 00:13:15,518
refs/pull/7/head is your commit.
refs/pull/7/merge is d04ef31.

192
00:13:15,558 --> 00:13:19,686
Compare it with every other
line: no branch points at it.

193
00:13:20,026 --> 00:13:23,794
The runner fetches that one
commit and detaches HEAD on it.

194
00:13:23,834 --> 00:13:29,216
git checkout --detach is the older spelling.
The script uses the git switch --detach

195
00:13:29,256 --> 00:13:30,700
form a few steps later.

196
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:35,888
Read the subject of the commit: "Merge",
your commit's full ID, "into", the ID of

197
00:13:35,928 --> 00:13:36,551
main's tip.

198
00:13:36,891 --> 00:13:41,683
On the runner now: what does git status
say about the branch, who is the author of

199
00:13:41,723 --> 00:13:45,925
HEAD, how many commits are there, and
what is the threshold in stock.py?

200
00:13:45,965 --> 00:13:46,868
Say it out loud.

201
00:13:51,708 --> 00:13:57,152
"HEAD (no branch)". The author is GitHub:
the commit nobody on the team wrote.

202
00:13:57,192 --> 00:14:02,917
One commit. No current branch. The threshold
is 10, from main, and report.py, from

203
00:14:02,957 --> 00:14:07,557
your branch, is there too. The
working tree contains both changes.

204
00:14:07,897 --> 00:14:10,760
The tests fail on the runner
and pass on your laptop.

205
00:14:11,100 --> 00:14:15,860
Both results are correct. They tested
different commits, and that's the CTO's first

206
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:16,958
question, answered.

207
00:14:17,298 --> 00:14:22,564
Back in your clone. You copy the commit ID
from the CI log and ask your clone about

208
00:14:22,604 --> 00:14:25,057
it. What happens? Make your prediction.

209
00:14:29,897 --> 00:14:33,349
"Could not get object info."
The commit isn't in your clone.

210
00:14:33,389 --> 00:14:37,830
Then the reproduction: fetch, detach on
your branch so that the branch itself

211
00:14:37,870 --> 00:14:42,661
doesn't move, and merge origin/main. git
merge is 🟡 CAUTION, and on a detached HEAD

212
00:14:42,701 --> 00:14:43,766
it moves only HEAD.

213
00:14:44,106 --> 00:14:48,891
The tree ID of your local merge equals
the tree ID on the runner, 745aaaa.

214
00:14:48,931 --> 00:14:53,259
Same tree, so the same test result:
the failure reproduces locally.

215
00:14:53,299 --> 00:14:57,431
Then the script switches back to
the branch, which is unchanged.

216
00:14:59,271 --> 00:15:00,919
Five mistakes to watch for.

217
00:15:01,259 --> 00:15:04,924
Silencing git describe with
--always in a release build.

218
00:15:04,964 --> 00:15:10,349
Root cause: with one commit and no tags
the command would fail, and --always turns

219
00:15:10,389 --> 00:15:14,848
that failure into a bare commit ID
that gets stamped as the version.

220
00:15:15,188 --> 00:15:20,103
Setting fetch-tags: true and keeping depth
1. Root cause: describe walks parents

221
00:15:20,143 --> 00:15:25,119
from HEAD to a tagged ancestor, and the
graft ends the walk before it gets there.

222
00:15:25,459 --> 00:15:30,453
Debugging a CI failure on your branch tip.
Root cause: a pull_request run checks out

223
00:15:30,493 --> 00:15:34,947
the test merge under refs/pull/N/merge,
a commit that is not in your clone.

224
00:15:35,287 --> 00:15:40,415
Re-running a job to "pick up the new main".
Root cause: a re-run reuses the original

225
00:15:40,455 --> 00:15:40,846
commit.

226
00:15:41,186 --> 00:15:45,597
Reading missing checks on a conflicted
pull request as a broken workflow.

227
00:15:45,637 --> 00:15:50,171
Root cause: a conflicted pull request
has no merge ref, so its pull_request

228
00:15:50,211 --> 00:15:51,879
workflows do not run at all.

229
00:15:53,719 --> 00:15:58,540
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's case:
an ML team's pull request adds a metric

230
00:15:58,580 --> 00:16:02,261
and passes locally. CI fails in
an unrelated data-loader test.

231
00:16:02,301 --> 00:16:07,062
The author spends an hour on the loader
before looking at the first lines of the

232
00:16:07,102 --> 00:16:11,023
checkout step's log, where the
fetched ref is refs/pull/412/merge.

233
00:16:11,063 --> 00:16:14,504
Someone had merged a loader
change twenty minutes earlier.

234
00:16:14,844 --> 00:16:19,747
The habit that prevents the lost hour: read
which commit the job checked out before

235
00:16:19,787 --> 00:16:24,274
reading anything else. Workflow 1 prints
it in its own step for that reason.

236
00:16:26,114 --> 00:16:31,600
Your turn. Do Lab 26.3, "Workflow
3, build the application", in

237
00:16:31,640 --> 00:16:36,951
lab-manual/m26-actions-fundamentals.md,
in your normal shell.

238
00:16:37,291 --> 00:16:42,001
The lab asks for two predictions. Write
both down. When the tag is pushed: which

239
00:16:42,041 --> 00:16:46,039
workflows start, and what will the
build job print after "Building"?

240
00:16:46,079 --> 00:16:50,493
And after one more commit lands on main:
what will the description be then?

241
00:16:50,833 --> 00:16:57,376
The challenge is Exercise 28.5, "Green
here, red in a fresh clone", in

242
00:16:57,416 --> 00:17:00,950
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

243
00:17:04,790 --> 00:17:09,567
Answer out loud. A strong answer describes
the repository on the runner as Git

244
00:17:09,607 --> 00:17:14,507
state: how many commits, which refs, what
HEAD is, and which file marks the cut.

245
00:17:14,547 --> 00:17:19,448
Then it picks three history questions and
classifies each as an error or a wrong

246
00:17:19,488 --> 00:17:23,647
answer, with the mechanism. It says
which is more dangerous and why.

247
00:17:23,687 --> 00:17:27,785
The follow-up is about fetch-tags:
true with the depth left at one.

248
00:17:27,825 --> 00:17:31,985
You've seen the answer, so explain
it from how describe finds a tag.

249
00:17:33,825 --> 00:17:34,752
Let's land this.

250
00:17:35,092 --> 00:17:36,752
You should now be able to say:

251
00:17:37,092 --> 00:17:42,360
After a default checkout the runner has one
commit, no tags, and a .git/shallow file

252
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:44,003
that cuts history at HEAD.

253
00:17:44,343 --> 00:17:49,293
History questions there either fail or give
a confident wrong answer; fetch-depth: 0

254
00:17:49,333 --> 00:17:51,729
in the job that needs history is the fix.

255
00:17:52,069 --> 00:17:56,602
A pull_request run checks out the test merge
under refs/pull/N/merge with a detached

256
00:17:56,642 --> 00:17:59,814
HEAD: a commit that is on the
server and not in your clone.

257
00:18:00,154 --> 00:18:05,155
To reproduce what CI tested, fetch and
merge the base into a detached copy of your

258
00:18:05,195 --> 00:18:06,938
branch, and compare tree IDs.

259
00:18:07,278 --> 00:18:12,079
A re-run reuses the original commit, and
a conflicted pull request gets no run at

260
00:18:12,119 --> 00:18:12,318
all.

261
00:18:14,158 --> 00:18:17,314
Read section 20A.8 of Chapter 20A.

262
00:18:17,654 --> 00:18:22,316
Today you rebuilt a runner's checkout on
your own machine, and caught one tool

263
00:18:22,356 --> 00:18:27,200
lying. Practise that once before you need
it. Next time: controlling jobs, matrix

264
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:32,143
strategies, dependency caching, and artifacts.
Until then, look at the state first

265
00:18:32,183 --> 00:18:34,615
and type second. See you in the next one.
